-3.2 For each corresponding pair of prime factor diagrams needed in that comparison, apply the imported theorem [F3] to the orientation-preserving pair map induced by its actual capped-factor isotopy. When an intermediate topological planar map $\phi:S^2\to S^2$ carries one decorated diagram to the next, it gives a finite graph isomorphism preserving the cyclic order at every crossing, the over-under data, and the oriented strand directions. Realize that same graph isomorphism smoothly as follows. Take disjoint crossing disks and thin edge strips for the two connected projection graphs. On each crossing disk choose an orientation-preserving smooth map carrying the four branch germs to their matched germs; on each edge strip choose a smooth monotone arc map that agrees with these maps on smaller endpoint collars. The connected graph's regular neighborhood has disk complements: thicken a spanning tree to a disk, then add the other edge strips one at a time. Each new edge lies in one old disk face and cuts it along a proper arc; the two rounded boundary curves are smooth Jordan curves whose disk sides, by [F7], are exactly the two new complementary disks. By [F7] extend the matched boundary maps over these disk faces with product collars. Gluing produces an orientation-preserving smooth sphere diffeomorphism $g$ inducing exactly $\phi$'s decorated graph map and component bijection. By [F8], $g$ is joined to the identity by a jointly smooth orientation-preserving isotopy. Thus each topological planar map supplied by [F3] is realized by a sphere planar isotopy in the definition, with the prescribed oriented component correspondence. [F3, F6, F7, F8, step 3.1, construct]
